An OpenAI-compatible LLM provider whose external integration configures no secret now sends a placeholder API key rather than none. litellm builds an OpenAI client for that provider type, and that client raises "Missing credentials" locally - before any request leaves the process - when it has no key at all, so the unauthenticated on-premises endpoint ADR 0006 makes a first-class case could not make a single call. A real OpenAI or Anthropic provider with no key still sends none, and fails with the provider's own message on the record. Also: an LLM provider's external integration no longer passes `ssl_verify` as a call keyword. litellm does not accept it and sweeps an unknown keyword into `extra_body`, so it went to the provider in the request JSON - meaning *SSL Verification* and *CA File Path* had no effect on LLM calls while appearing to, and a stray field rode along with every request. litellm reads TLS settings process-wide only, and writing its global per call would let one provider disable verification on another's concurrent connection, so the app applies neither and logs a warning naming what it skipped and the `SSL_VERIFY`/`SSL_CERT_FILE` variables to use instead. MCP servers are unaffected: `services/mcp.py` builds its own HTTP client per call and does honour both.
